<Docs edit="site/src/pages/docs/concepts/firmware.astro" title="How the firmware works" description="What runs on the panel, and how it talks to Home Assistant and the rest of your network." group="Concepts" current={url('/docs/concepts/firmware/')} headings={headings}>
  <h2 id="one-renderer">One renderer, two places</h2>
  <p>
    Facet is one renderer, written in C++ on <a href="https://lvgl.io">LVGL</a>. The same source builds two ways: into
    the panel's firmware, and into WebAssembly for the browser. These docs use the browser build, so the components and
    screens you see here are drawn pixel for pixel as the panel draws them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="panel">What runs on the panel</h2>
  <p>
    The firmware is an <a href="https://esphome.io">ESPHome</a> build. ESPHome looks after the hardware, Wi-Fi and the
    link to Home Assistant; Facet is an ESPHome component inside it that draws every screen, handles every tap and plays
    the sounds. Taps are handled on the panel itself, so moving between screens needs no round trip to anything else.
  </p>
  <p>
    A release carries only the screens that are ready. Screens still being made are left out of the firmware, so the
    panel never shows a half-built screen or a card that leads nowhere.
  </p>

  <h2 id="home-assistant">Talking to Home Assistant</h2>
  <p>
    The panel uses ESPHome's own connection to Home Assistant, encrypted with a key Home Assistant makes when you add the
    panel. Over it the panel:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>reads</strong> the state of the entities its screens show, and updates each card as they change;</li>
    <li><strong>calls</strong> Home Assistant actions when you use a control, such as switching a light (this needs the panel to be allowed to perform actions);</li>
    <li><strong>shows up</strong> as a device with its own entities: its settings, the firmware update, Wi-Fi signal and more.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="own-data">Data the panel gets itself</h2>
  <p>
    Some screens need data Home Assistant may not have, such as the weather, the moon or energy prices. The panel fetches
    these from public services itself, and only while that screen is on show.
  </p>

  <h2 id="screens">Screens and the deck</h2>
  <p>
    Every screen is a JSON spec, a tree of <a href={url('/docs/concepts/components/')}>components</a>. The panel's set of
    Homes, cards and screens is its <em>deck</em>, kept on the panel. It starts with Facet's own, and changes you make in
    <strong>Settings &gt; Screens</strong>, or a deck sent to it, are stored on the panel and kept across restarts.
    <a href={url('/docs/home-cards/')}>Home cards</a> explains the cards.
  </p>

  <h2 id="api">The panel API</h2>
  <p>
    The panel also answers a small HTTP API on your network: what it runs, what it can show, and a way to show a screen
    or install a new one. Anything that changes the panel, except from the panel's own pages, needs the
    <strong>Allow AI agents</strong> setting on (it is off by default) and the panel's token.
    <a href={url('/ai/')}>Make screens with AI</a> describes it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="updates">Updates</h2>
  <p>
    The panel checks for new releases itself and offers them in Home Assistant; it installs only when you say so, and
    goes back to the previous version if an update does not settle. <a href={url('/docs/troubleshooting/')}>Updating and
    troubleshooting</a> has the details.
  </p>
</Docs>
